                <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
                <div class="mt-16">
                  <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
                    Common Concrete Countertop Sealing Mistakes
                  </h2>

                  <p>
                    A countertop is the hardest concrete surface to seal well: it meets acid, heat, and standing water daily, and every flaw sits at eye level in good light.
                  </p>

                  <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Any Sealer Is Food-Safe</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Food-safe is a specific claim, usually tied to NSF or FDA compliance once fully cured, and most general-purpose concrete sealers do not carry it. On a food-preparation surface, check for the certification explicitly rather than inferring it from a product being sold for countertops.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a Penetrating Sealer for a Kitchen</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Penetrating sealers repel water without forming a film, which is ideal outdoors and inadequate against lemon juice, vinegar, and wine. Acid etches concrete straight through a penetrating sealer. Kitchen countertops need a film-forming topical sealer to stand a chance against everyday acids.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Before the Concrete Has Cured</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Fresh concrete keeps releasing moisture and remains highly alkaline for weeks, and both will cloud or lift a sealer applied too early. Most countertop sealers want a minimum of 28 days of cure. Rushing this is the most common cause of a hazy, blotchy finish that has to be stripped and redone.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating It as a Permanent Finish</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Even the best countertop sealers wear at the sink, the cooktop side, and wherever a cutting board lands. A concrete countertop is a maintained surface, not a sealed-once one. Plan on periodic re-coating and a wax or conditioner between, and it will look good for years.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>

